  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 925 PM MST Sat Nov 14 2026\n\nDRIER CONDITIONS FOR SUNDAY & MONDAY:\n\nOvernight cooling after the recent precipitation could lead to\npockets of patchy fog early Sunday morning in the lower valleys\nof eastern Utah and western Colorado. Aside from that, the \nprevious shortwave trough has moved eastward, leaving us with \ngenerally dry and mild conditions to start the week. The \nbackside of the low will leave some lingering cloud cover in \nsome places, but daytime highs are still expected to run a few \ndegrees above normal. \n\nNEXT SYSTEM ARRIVES TUESDAY:\n\nOur focus then shifts to another shortwave trough moving through the \narea from Tuesday afternoon through Wednesday. While this system \nwill be slightly cooler, the bulk of the precipitation is still \nexpected to fall ahead of the cold front. As a result, snowfall \ntotals should look similar to the previous system, peaking at 4-8\" \nin the southern mountains, with lighter amounts farther north. Snow \nlevels will also be hovering around 7-9 kft. Behind the front, \ntemperatures will drop to near or slightly below normal for much of \nthe week. \n\nLATE WEEK SYSTEM: \n\nForecast models continue to show another system arriving late this \nweek on Friday and Saturday, though trends are shifting toward an \nopen wave setup. Because the models remain inconsistent, confidence \nin the exact outcome is still low. This shift has also caused \nprojected precipitation totals to trend downward over recent runs. \nMost of the moisture is expected to arrive ahead of the cold front, \nmaking timing critical. If the precipitation and front line up just \nright, snow levels could drop to some valley floors Friday night \ninto Saturday morning. For now, however, the specifics remain \nuncertain.",
